## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to enforce tenancy isolation and data minimization in prompts and outputs, plus post-checks before delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented prompts, tenancy scoping, redaction, post-filtering, test harness concepts.

## Code Example
```javascript
function wrapPrompt(tenantId, userPrompt) {
  // basic tenant-scoped redaction and prefix
  const safePrompt = `[TENANT:${tenantId}] PROMPT: ${userPrompt}`;
  return safePrompt;
}

function mockLLM(prompt){
  // simulate a model that may leak data
  return `Response for ${prompt} with tenant id ${prompt.match(/TENANT:(\\w+)/)[1]}`;
}

function postCheck(response, tenantId){
  // naive leakage check
  if (response.includes(tenantId)) return null;
  return response;
}

console.log(postCheck(mockLLM(wrapPrompt('T123','List logs for user emails')),
  'T123'));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve this for real-world tenancy boundaries and performance at scale?
- What tests would you add to catch redaction misses and leakage?
- How would you handle dynamic redaction rules per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a modular, latency-aware prompt routing flow in an edge/cloud context. It requires turning a user goal into a module selection, a concise execution plan, and a concrete payload while considering safety and edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt routing and modular design
- PII redaction and latency constraints
- Edge-case handling and fallbacks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny router sketch
function routePrompt(req){
  const intent = req.intent||'';
  const data = req.data_sensitivity||'';
  const latency = req.latency_budget||Infinity;
  if(intent.includes('summary') && data==='PII' && latency<=1000){
    return {module:'Summary-Builder', plan:['fetch','redact','summarize'], payload:{regions:req.regions||['us-east','us-west'], redact:true}};
  }
  return {module:'Data-Extractor', plan:['extract'], payload:{}};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this for latency budgets and safety guards?
- How would you extend to handle parallel modules and partial results?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a configurable policy framework that adapts prompts based on language and content risk, with traceable moderation reasoning and backpressure.

## Key Concepts
- Language detection and content classification
- Policy routing matrix (per-language, per-category)
- Guardrails and re-prompt loops
- Audit logging for moderation workflows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype: routePrompt(prompt, lang, category) -> {policy, rePrompt}
function routePrompt(prompt, lang, category){
  const policyKey = (category in policyMap && lang in policyMap[category]) ? policyMap[category][lang] : 'default';
  let rePrompt = null;
  const risk = scoreRisk(prompt, category, lang);
  if(risk > threshold){
    rePrompt = `Please comply with ${policyKey} before proceeding.`;
  }
  return {policy: policyKey, rePrompt};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language policy consistency?
- How would you store and audit re-prompt decisions at scale?
